# Post-merge review

This is the project's second review: a re-review, at review-tier (`.forge/orchestration.md`; `.forge/model-tiers.md` for the model), of every PR merged to `main`.
It catches the class of bug a per-PR diff review structurally can't see: cross-PR, migration and resource-exhaustion-under-execution bugs that only show up across several merged PRs together.
It sits beside `code-review` and `self-review` as the project's standing review tooling.

**The post-merge review tracker {{ pmr_ref }}** holds the reviews still to be done and the reviews done (SDLC-095): a pending note for each merged PR no run has covered yet, the watermark, the run log, and the findings each run files as its sub-issues.
This file is the single source of truth for the procedure; don't let a copy drift into the issue body, where size limits and edits lose it.

**This is file-only**: a run opens issues and never opens fix PRs.

## Pending reviews

When a PR merges, the agent that merged it posts one comment on the tracker {{ pmr_ref }}, as the merge's last step.
A spec-only PR and a lifecycle-document PR are now ordinary agent-merged PRs once the owner's approval of their wording is recorded (`.forge/process-and-evidence.md`, "What needs the owner's approval before a merge"; SDLC-043), so the agent that merges one posts the note itself, as line 20 says.
When the owner or the person merges a PR an agent opened instead (an `implement` PR, where the owner chose that route, or a release-notes PR), the agent that opened it posts the note once it sees the merge:

```
<!-- pending-post-merge-review pr=<PR number> sha=<merge commit sha> -->
Pending post-merge review: PR #<PR number> for #<ticket number>, merged as <short sha>.
```

The note is a comment, never an edit to the tracker's body and never a sub-issue, because sub-issues are findings.
A note is pending until a run's archive comment (step 7) lists its PR as covered; that list is the record, not the per-PR verdicts.
The notes are the review's input record; the watermark query (step 1) still decides the window, and the notes cross-check it.
**Trust a pending note only when its comment's `author_association` is `OWNER`, `MEMBER` or `COLLABORATOR`.**
On a public repository anyone can comment, and an unauthenticated note could add a PR to the review window that was never actually merged by the project's own agents, or claim coverage it doesn't have; step 1's watermark query filters on this the same way.

## Grading rules

- **Grade against the project's real scale and tenancy, never the demo's.** Never downgrade an authorization, isolation or cross-tenant finding because "only one tenant exists today" or "it looks fine for one user"; grade it against what the requirements say the system must support.
- **For any test asserting a security guard, permission check or other invariant (new or pre-existing), check that it actually fails when the guard is removed or neutered.**
  Don't read the test and judge it plausible; verify it directly (comment out the guard, re-run, confirm red, restore it) before crediting it as real coverage.
  A guard test that can't fail this way is itself a Must finding, not a pass.
- **Concurrency and lifecycle checklist.** Run it whenever a surviving line touches connections, pools, background tasks, sockets, locks, slots or claims, or per-session or per-connection cached state; it is the same checklist `.agents/skills/self-review/SKILL.md`'s "Concurrency and lifecycle checklist (triggered)" runs before merge:
  1. **Teardown:** for each connection, pool, task or subscription the surviving lines create, what it does on shutdown, cancellation and error, and who closes it.
  2. **Fire-and-forget failure:** does a failing lookup or exception in a background path abort work it shouldn't, or vanish silently?
  3. **Stale cached state:** state cached per connection or per session that a membership, permission or ownership change can make stale; who invalidates it?
  4. **Release on every exit:** each slot, claim, lock or counter the surviving lines take is released on every path out (return, raise, cancel, timeout).
  5. **Ordering under concurrency:** two requests interleaving at each `await` the surviving lines add.
  6. **Operation state across an identity switch:** a pending operation whose user, account or workspace changes before it completes is cancelled or dropped, never applied to the new context.
- Everything else follows the MoSCoW definitions in `.agents/skills/code-review/SKILL.md`.

## Security axis

Each pass also reviews every surviving line through STRIDE (Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information disclosure, Denial of service, Elevation of privilege), against `docs/security/threat-model.md` and every other doc under `docs/security/`, alongside the grading rules above.
For every surviving line, ask:

- did it add attack surface (a new endpoint, tool, input path, external call, stored data or secret)?
- did it weaken a control one of those docs names?
- did it make a statement in one of those docs untrue?
- did it skip the Security part of its design-exit Structure guidance or its design-doc slice's threat-model delta?

**The reviewer finds gaps and doesn't fill them.** Findings use the same MoSCoW triage and themed-bundle filing rules as every other finding (step 6); a Must about a security control still gets its own ticket, never folded into a bundle.
Distinguish two shapes of finding:

- The **code** is the problem (a weakened or missing control, new attack surface the threat model doesn't cover, a skipped Security part): grade and file it like any other finding.
- A **security doc** is the one out of date (the code is fine, but a doc's statement about it no longer holds): file it as a **Should** (never a Must, since nothing is broken) whose ticket points at `.agents/skills/security-docs/SKILL.md`'s reconcile mode.
  The pass never edits the doc itself, the same as it never edits code; bundle it with the run's other Should findings.

Name this axis explicitly in each review sub-agent's brief (step 4); a brief that omits it gets a review that skips security entirely.
Record in the run's archive comment (step 7) which `docs/security/` files were checked.

**Merge-written text is in scope too.** A PR's title and description, the commit messages a squash or merge writes, and the author/committer identity on each of those commits can all carry text that never appears as a line in any file's diff — so the per-file surviving-line review in step 3 never sees any of it. For every PR in the window, read its title and body and, for the commits that landed for it, both the message and the identity (`git log --format='%H%n%an <%ae>%n%cn <%ce>%n%B%n---' <sha>..origin/main`, substituting the watermark line's own `<sha>`; the trailing `---` line separates one commit's output from the next, since `%B` can itself contain blank lines), and ask: did any of it disclose something that shouldn't be public (a personal address or name on a commit author/committer line, not only in the message body), or carry a closing keyword next to the wrong issue or PR reference? Grade a finding here like any other. The fix for text already on `main` is the project's own call (editing a PR body where the platform allows it, a history rewrite) — this pass files the finding and never applies that fix itself.

## Cadence

Run a pass after roughly every 10 PRs merged to `main`, and at least every 2-3 days of active development, whichever comes first.
The Main Health thread runs this on its own routine (`.agents/skills/watch-main-ci/SKILL.md`, Routine 1), skipping a firing when nothing has merged since the watermark, plus on request near the end of a feature pipeline or remediation epic, and whenever a feature's `Post-merge review pass for <feature>` ticket is unblocked.
Every 5th pass (run number divisible by 5) also runs step 5a's tautological-test sample, behind its own owner gate.

**Find candidate PRs**

```
repo:{{ repo_owner }}/{{ repo_name }} is:pr is:merged merged:>WATERMARK-ISO-8601
```

Substitute the `merged <ISO8601>` timestamp from the watermark line currently recorded on the tracker {{ pmr_ref }} (step 9 defines its one canonical format).

## Procedure

The pass reviews files on current `main`, not PRs, so nothing gets reviewed twice and nothing dead gets reviewed:

1. Run the query above to list PRs merged since the watermark (or, more reliably, first-parent `git log <sha>..origin/main`, substituting the watermark line's own `<sha>`).
   **Resolve each commit in that window to its PR with `scripts/detect_pr_for_commit.py {{ repo_owner }}/{{ repo_name }} <sha>`, never by parsing its title or message.** It runs the `merge_commit_sha` lookup (`gh api repos/{{ repo_owner }}/{{ repo_name }}/commits/<sha>/pulls`) and exits 1, flagged, only when no PR matches *and* the committer isn't GitHub's own merge identity (`GitHub <noreply@github.com>`/`web-flow`); title or message text (a `(#N)` suffix, an issue number) is never consulted, since that misreads a real PR's squash commit as a direct-to-main bypass whenever the merger supplied a custom squash title with no such suffix. A flagged commit is the real signal of a direct-to-main bypass: file it as its own `needs-decision` finding, never silently folded into this run's ordinary window.
   Re-review **every** PR in the window; this pass exists for cross-PR bugs a per-PR review can't see.
   Then list the pending notes ("Pending reviews" above) that no archive comment has yet covered, and check each noted PR is in the window.
   Add a noted PR the query missed to the window, unless an earlier run's verdicts already reviewed it, in which case list its note as covered in step 7 without reviewing it again.
   Review a PR in the window that has no note all the same, post its missing note, and record it as unnoted in step 7.
2. Build the file set: for every PR in the window, list the files it touched, and take the union across all of them, deduplicated.
   This is the review surface for this run; nothing outside it gets touched.
3. For each file in that set, run `git blame origin/main` on it and keep only the lines whose blamed commit belongs to one of this run's PRs (cross-reference the commit against `git log origin/main --format='%h %P %s'` for the PR's merge or squash commit).
   These are the **surviving lines**, the only ones any in-window PR still contributes to that file today; lines a later, out-of-window commit has since replaced are superseded and never reviewed.
   - In a shallow clone, `git fetch --unshallow` first; a shallow boundary grafts some merge commits as parentless roots and drops their blame entirely.
   - If a file has zero surviving lines for every in-window PR that touched it, record that in the archive comment (step 7) instead of reviewing it.
   - A two-parent merge commit's own file-change set is `git diff <parent1> <parent2> --name-only`, not `git show`, and blame on a line it brought in may attribute to one of the merged branch's own commits, which still counts as in-window.
   - Squash-merged commit subjects may carry the ticket number rather than the PR number; resolve a squash commit to its PR via the API, the same lookup step 1 uses, rather than trusting the number in the subject.
4. Batch by subsystem (each top-level source area, tests, infrastructure, docs), not by PR order, so each reviewer keeps one coherent area of context.
   Run one research-only reviewer per batch, dispatched in parallel where the harness can, at review-tier; the coordinating thread running the pass stays at build-tier.
   A batch that is purely mechanical (docs-only, small verbatim extractions) can instead be dispatched at mechanical-tier; substantive batches default to review-tier.
   - Each review sub-agent **must report which model it actually ran on**; don't trust a tier alias silently.
     It also writes its report incrementally to the working folder ({{ working_folder }}) as it goes, so a restart mid-batch doesn't lose the work.
   - Every review-tier reviewer's brief opens with this tier gate, before any of the batch content below, as the first thing that sub-agent does. This is post-merge-review's own version of `.forge/orchestration.md`'s "Tier gate": `get_session` with no id reports the *orchestrating* (parent) session's model, not a dispatched sub-agent's own, so it cannot verify this sub-agent's tier and is not used as the check (owner decision; a mechanical-tier batch is dispatched at that tier on purpose and carries no gate):

     > Before any other work, state the model actually serving this turn, read from your own system prompt, and check it against what `.forge/model-tiers.md` maps this tier to.
     > This self-report is the check and the recorded evidence; `get_session` with no id reports the orchestrating session's model, not yours, so don't use it to decide whether to proceed. If you call it anyway and it disagrees with your self-report, note the mismatch in your report without acting on it.
     > If your self-reported model is below the mapped model, do no other work on this ticket or stage: report to the coordinator and the owner that this thread must be restarted on the right model, and stop there.
     > A thread cannot switch its own model, which is why the gate stops and reports rather than continuing or switching itself.
     > Running on a model above the mapped one is not a problem: note it in one line and proceed.
   - Give each reviewer: its batch's file list; each file's surviving-line ranges and which PRs contributed each range; the contributing PRs' unresolved review threads for those ranges, to verify independently rather than trust; the MoSCoW definitions, the grading rules and the security axis above, naming the security axis explicitly; an instruction to review each file exactly once, covering every surviving line and crediting each finding to the PRs whose lines it falls in; an instruction to confirm every finding is still present on current `origin/main`, cite file and line, and include a repro where feasible; and an instruction not to post anything or edit any code.
   - If no sub-agent dispatch is available, don't silently substitute a single-session partial pass: the archive comment says exactly which files and subsystems were covered and which were left, and the watermark doesn't advance past the uncovered portion.
   - **Merge-written text is its own batch, not split across the subsystem batches above.** It isn't file-scoped, so giving every batch the whole window's PR titles, bodies, commit messages and commit identities would review each one several times over. Dispatch one reviewer at review-tier over the window's merge-written text, giving it every in-window PR's title and body plus `git log --format='%H%n%an <%ae>%n%cn <%ce>%n%B%n---' <sha>..origin/main` (substituting the watermark line's own `<sha>`), the security axis's merge-written-text question above — naming author and committer identity explicitly as part of it, not only the message body — and the same MoSCoW and grading rules as the other batches.
   - **Dispatch each batch against its own private worktree**, never the thread's shared checkout, whenever a local full-suite run or another batch might be running there.
   - **The window can grow mid-run.** Before filing or advancing the watermark, re-fetch and compare the window with what every batch actually covered.
     Either check a small, well-understood gap directly, or leave substantial newly merged PRs for the next pass; either way, never advance the watermark past a PR no batch covered.
     When a finding might already be fixed by a newly merged PR, re-verify against the live `origin/main` tip before filing.
5. Before filing, spot-check every Must and a sample of Shoulds against the code.
   When `main` is red, run the one test that covers the finding by itself before briefing reviewers on it; a single test run in isolation can implicate a different test than a fuller run does.

5a. **Tautological-test sample (every 5th pass).** Runs only when this pass's run number is a multiple of 5; otherwise the archive comment says "5a: not due".
    This step never gates or delays the watermark, and a declined or partial sample doesn't make the pass partial.

    1. **Select the sample.** List the test files added or modified in the window (`git diff --name-only --diff-filter=AM <watermark>..origin/main` filtered to the project's test directories).
       Map each changed test file to the production module it covers (by naming convention, or by running that test file alone under coverage and taking the module with the most covered lines).
       Rank modules by test lines changed in the window and take **up to 3 modules**, including at least one per language when the window changed tests in more than one.
       An empty diff means "5a: no tests changed in window".
    2. **Owner gate.** Never run a mutation tool without checking with the owner first, every time (`.agents/skills/tdd/tests.md`).
       Before installing or running anything, put one decision card to the owner naming the modules, the sampled test files, the tool and version (`docs/agents/testing-tools.md`), and the time cap, with options "Run it" and "Skip this pass".
       Continue steps 6-9 without waiting.
       An answer of "Run it" before the next pass runs steps 3-5 below as an addendum; no answer by the next due pass means the sample lapses ("5a: no owner answer").
       Never run without a yes on *that pass's* card.
    3. **Run it** in a private worktree off `origin/main`, against a private throwaway database or fixture store created for this run and dropped afterwards, never a shared one.
       Check no local full-suite run is in progress, and don't start while step 4's reviewers are still running.
       Use the project's pinned mutation tool for each language, limited to the sampled module and its sampled tests.
       Bound each module to 20 minutes of wall clock and the whole sample to 60; a module that hits the cap is reported with partial counts and "timed out", not retried.
       If the tool cannot run the sampled tests cleanly, do 3 hand mutations for that module (mutate, run the one test, revert) and file one Should naming the test file; don't debug tooling inside a review pass. This fallback is still this gated, tool-driven mutation-testing sample, not guard-removal verification (`.agents/skills/tdd/SKILL.md`): it's a module-wide sample run under step 5a's owner gate, never the routine per-guard check build threads do every ticket.
    4. **Decide what is a finding.** A sampled test that covers at least one mutant and kills none is a finding.
       Two tests at the same seam that kill exactly the same mutants are *candidates* for duplication, never a deletion order; keep the clearer one unless the other encodes a past regression.
       Surviving mutants no sampled test covers are coverage gaps, not findings; record only the count.
    5. **File and record.** Findings are Should by default, bundled by module area under step 6; a surviving test that claims to cover a security guard or invariant is a Must with its own ticket.
       The ticket carries the mutation evidence; the fix ticket rewrites or deletes the tests, never this step.
       Add a "5a sample" block to the archive comment: modules, tool and version, mutants generated, killed, survived and timed out per module, wall time, and findings with ticket links.

6. File the findings, bundled by theme rather than one ticket per finding:
   - **Must findings each get their own ticket**, kept apart from any bundle, so they stay fast to pick up.
   - **Should findings are grouped by file or feature area into one ticket per theme for this run.** A theme is whatever natural grouping the run's batches produced; don't split a theme just to keep tickets small.
   - **A genuine open design question with no fix sketch is flagged to the owner and gets its own `deferred-arch-finding` ticket** under the architecture-findings epic {{ arch_ref }}, instead of a bundle or a Could one-liner.
   - **If an open sub-issue already covers the same feature area, add the new findings to it as a comment** instead of opening a new ticket.
   - Otherwise, create a new sub-issue under the tracker {{ pmr_ref }}, per Must or per theme, labelled `ready-for-build` or `ready-for-structure` per `docs/agents/triage-labels.md`'s routing criteria (on any doubt, `ready-for-structure`).
   - Every Must or Should ticket asks for the fix PR's `Evidence:` line to list by name the other members of the finding's class it checked, and the source that list came from (the code that handles the cases, or the list, schema or specification that defines them), not only the examples the ticket names.
   - Could and Won't findings never get their own ticket; they go only in this run's archive comment as one-liners.
   - **Two or more filed findings with the same shape** (the same kind of mistake, wherever it sits): as well as filing them, put one decision card per shape to the owner asking "Is there a principle here?".
     The card names the shape, the tickets that carry it, and a one-line candidate statement; if `docs/principles.md` already has a principle the shape breaks, it asks instead whether that principle's enforcement should be tightened.
     Options: "Add it as a principle" and "No, the tickets cover it"; carry on without waiting.
     A yes is queued as a `ready-for-build` ticket whose build adds or tightens the entry through `domain-modeling`; this procedure never edits `docs/principles.md` itself.
7. Post one comment on the tracker {{ pmr_ref }} for the run: the per-PR verdicts (its verdict and which sub-issue it fed), the pending notes it covered and any it left pending, any in-window PR that had no note, the Could and Won't list, any same-shape card from step 6 with the owner's answer once given, which `docs/security/` files the security axis checked, and a **Reviewers** block.
   The Reviewers block has one row per batch, naming the subsystem (or "merge-written text" for that batch), the sub-agent's id, its tier, and the model it reported running on; this is the only place that detail lives, never the issue body.
   Lead the comment with a machine-readable marker, `<!-- post-merge-review-archive covered=<PR number>,<PR number>,... -->`, listing every PR this run marks covered (the ones with a verdict in this run, plus any already-reviewed note re-covered per the "Pending reviews" section above) — never the PRs left pending or the ones with no note at all.
   This is what `queue-sweep` check 11(a) reads to tell a real archive comment apart from any other comment on the same tracker (a pending note, or a differently-shaped note such as `watch-main-ci` Routine 2's missing-script note) without relying on elimination, and to check a specific PR's note against rather than only the comment's own timestamp.
   Save any repro script written to confirm a finding in the working folder ({{ working_folder }}), under a per-run directory.
8. Comment once on each merged PR that has Must or Should findings, pointing to its sub-issue.
9. Update the **watermark** to `**Watermark:** <sha> (PR #<n>, merged <ISO8601>)` — the canonical line every other step and file in this family reads, carrying the commit, the PR number and that PR's own merge timestamp together so nothing downstream needs a second lookup for any of the three. Append one row to the **run log** table in the tracker's body linking to this run's archive comment.
   Mark a PR covered once every surviving line it contributed has been reviewed in this or an earlier run, or superseded by a later commit.
   **A run that did not cover its whole window must not advance the watermark**; it still appends its run log row, marked partial, so the gap is visible.

## A repeat offender becomes a self-review pattern

When a run finds the same kind of defect earlier runs already found, the pattern is added to `self-review`'s checklist in the same follow-up that files the findings, so the builder's own review catches it next time.
When a pattern recurs across rotations of the Main Health thread, it is also noted in the project's Main Health patterns notes (`.forge/main-health-patterns.md`).

## Keeping the tracker tidy

The tracker's native sub-issue list is the source of truth for what's open and closed; don't duplicate it in the body.
The body carries only the watermark, the procedure pointer and the run log index; pending notes and each run's detail go in comments, and findings are sub-issues, so never add a per-PR or per-finding table to the body.

If the tracker approaches the platform's sub-issue limit, move its closed sub-issues to an archive issue (titled `Post-merge review archive <n>`) to free room, and open the next archive issue once the current one fills.
Record the current archive issue's number in the tracker's body.

## Operational safety rules

These apply to every review sub-agent this procedure dispatches, and to the coordinating thread itself:

- **Never `pkill -f` or `pgrep -f`.** A pattern match can catch another session's process on a shared machine.
- **Shared services may be started, never stopped.** Other sessions may depend on a database or service staying up.
- **Never mutate a shared development or test database by hand** (drop, truncate, or edit rows); use a private throwaway one for anything destructive.
- **No paid external API calls (model providers, paid services) without the owner's explicit, per-run ask.**
- **Don't run review sub-agents concurrently with a local full-suite run**; the combined load causes timeouts that look like failures (`.agents/skills/watch-main-ci/SKILL.md`, "Overlap rule").
- **A step 5a mutation run is test load**: it follows the overlap rule, uses its own throwaway data, and never runs without the owner's yes on that pass's card.
